  "codex_feedback_round1": "VERDICT: NEEDS_REVISION\nSubmitted body is mostly sound and transferable, but it is truncated at the end and omits promotion-critical guardrails: concrete code anchor, P-P77 non-conflict boundary, determinism conditions, and delayed-consumer hard-do-not-apply cases. Promote the revised version below.\n\nREVISED_TITLE:\nGM workspace slot rotation via modulo-(MAX_LAG+1) for cross-core stage decoupling\n\nREVISED_BODY:\n`applies_to: any soc with cross-core sync; cann=9.0.0+; op_class=multi_stage_pipeline_with_GM_handoff`\n`derived-from: cann-source (FA-class workspace layout, 2026-05-10 revise-cl4)`\n`verified_on: cann-source (FA-class op_kernel main scheduling loop)`\n`unverified_on: a5_ops`\n\n**Trigger**: A multi-stage producer-consumer pipeline uses GM-resident workspace tensors as the hand-off medium between cores, such as cube-to-vec, vec-to-cube, or peer stage handoff, and adjacent stages must overlap without an iteration overwriting GM scratch that a downstream stage is still consuming.\n\n**Parameter definition**: `MAX_LAG` is the maximum iteration distance between a producer write of a slot and the last still-active consumer read of that same slot. It is not the count of stages in flight. Example: with three pipeline stages, stage-A writes iteration `k`, stage-B reads `k-1`, and stage-C reads `k-2`; this requires `MAX_LAG = 2` and therefore `MAX_LAG + 1 = 3` slots.\n\n**Recommendation**: Allocate `(MAX_LAG + 1)` parallel GM slots per core per logical workspace tensor. At outer-loop iteration `k`, the producer writes:\n\n```cpp\nslotIdx = k % (MAX_LAG + 1);\n```\n\nA consumer that reads data produced `lag` iterations earlier reads:\n\n```cpp\nreadSlot = (k + (MAX_LAG + 1) - lag) % (MAX_LAG + 1);\n```\n\nThe active set `{k, k-1, ..., k-MAX_LAG}` is pairwise-distinct modulo `MAX_LAG + 1`, so active producer/consumer generations do not alias. The slot from iteration `k - (MAX_LAG + 1)` is the one being overwritten at `k`; reuse is safe only after every consumer of that older generation has released it.\n\n**Safety condition**: This scheme is collision-free only when `MAX_LAG` is a structural upper bound on actual consumer retention and each slot region has exactly one producer before reuse. A producer at iteration `k + MAX_LAG + 1` must be gated by the last downstream consumer's release/consumed event for iteration `k`, not merely by producer-complete flags. If the schedule can let a consumer retain a slot beyond `MAX_LAG`, modulo rotation is unsafe.\n\n**Per-core GM offset**:\n\n```cpp\nslotOffset = uint64_t(coreIdx) * SLOT_BYTES * (MAX_LAG + 1)\n           + uint64_t(slotIdx) * SLOT_BYTES;\n```\n\n**Concrete anchor**:\n\n```cpp\nconstexpr uint32_t MAX_LAG = 2;\nconstexpr uint32_t SLOT_COUNT = MAX_LAG + 1;\n\nuint32_t writeSlot = iter % SLOT_COUNT;\nuint64_t writeOffset = uint64_t(coreIdx) * SLOT_BYTES * SLOT_COUNT\n                     + uint64_t(writeSlot) * SLOT_BYTES;\n\n// Consumer reads data produced `lag` iterations earlier.\nuint32_t readSlot = (iter + SLOT_COUNT - lag) % SLOT_COUNT;\nuint64_t readOffset = uint64_t(coreIdx) * SLOT_BYTES * SLOT_COUNT\n                    + uint64_t(readSlot) * SLOT_BYTES;\n```\n\nTypical `MAX_LAG = 2` gives three slots per core and supports a three-stage overlapped window. `MAX_LAG = 1` reduces to classic double-buffer ping-pong. `MAX_LAG >= 3` is rarely useful because the cross-core flag chain and slowest stage usually cap overlap; justify larger values with profiling.\n\n**Cross-reference**: This is GM-tier cross-core slot rotation. It does not conflict with P-P77, which rotates UB-resident `TQue` slots for same-core pipe synchronization. Kernels may use P-P77 inside a stage and this GM rotation between stages.\n\n**Determinism**: Deterministic when each `(coreIdx, workspaceTensor, slotIdx)` has one writer for a live generation, every consumer read is gated by producer-complete for the matching iteration, and slot reuse is gated by last-consumer release. This pattern does not make multi-producer writes deterministic; partition workspace per producer or use a separate deterministic merge.\n\n**Hard do-not-apply**:\n- Do not use bare modulo rotation when consumer retention is not bounded by `MAX_LAG`.\n- Do not rely only on producer-complete flags if producers can wrap before consumers release the slot.\n- Do not apply unchanged to data-dependent skip/backpressure schedules where downstream stages can fall behind by more than `MAX_LAG`.\n\nMitigations: increase slot count to a proven retention bound, add per-slot release/ack flags, or replace the modulo ring with explicit credit accounting.\n\n**Other instances predicted**:\n- FlashAttention-class forward GM scratch rotation.\n- Prefill/decode hybrid attention with fixed-lag stage handoff.\n- Multi-stage scan/reduction where partials are finalized later.\n- Fused norm-then-GEMM where vec scratch feeds a later cube stage.\n- MoE routing/finalize pipelines with fixed-lag scratch consumption.",
  "codex_feedback_round2": "VERDICT: NEEDS_REVISION\nCandidate is promotable in principle, but needs revision before canonical promotion because the body is truncated at the end, and `coreIdx` in the consumer offset is ambiguous for peer/core handoff. The canonical version should state that offsets are keyed by the producer/slot-owner core, and multi-consumer release must be aggregated before reuse.\n\nREVISED_TITLE:\nGM workspace ring-slot rotation via modulo-(MAX_LAG+1) for cross-core stage decoupling\nREVISED_BODY:\n`applies_to: any soc with cross-core sync; cann=9.0.0+; op_class=multi_stage_pipeline_with_GM_handoff`\n`derived-from: cann-source (FA-class workspace layout, 2026-05-10 revise-cl4)`\n`verified_on: cann-source (FA-class op_kernel main scheduling loop)`\n`unverified_on: a5_ops`\n\n**Trigger**: A multi-stage producer-consumer pipeline uses GM-resident workspace tensors as the hand-off medium between cores, such as cube-to-vec, vec-to-cube, or peer stage handoff, and adjacent stages must overlap without an iteration overwriting GM scratch that a downstream stage is still consuming.\n\n**Parameter definition**: `MAX_LAG` is the maximum iteration distance between a producer write of a slot and the last still-active consumer read of that same slot. It is not the count of stages in flight. Example: with three pipeline stages, stage-A writes iteration `k`, stage-B reads `k-1`, and stage-C reads `k-2`; this requires `MAX_LAG = 2` and therefore `MAX_LAG + 1 = 3` slots.\n\n**Recommendation**: Allocate `(MAX_LAG + 1)` parallel GM slots per producer/slot-owner core per logical workspace tensor. At outer-loop iteration `k`, the producer writes:\n\n```cpp\nslotIdx = k % (MAX_LAG + 1);\n```\n\nA consumer that reads data produced `lag` iterations earlier reads:\n\n```cpp\nreadSlot = (k + (MAX_LAG + 1) - lag) % (MAX_LAG + 1);\n```\n\nRequire `0 <= lag <= MAX_LAG`. The active set `{k, k-1, ..., k-MAX_LAG}` is pairwise-distinct modulo `MAX_LAG + 1`, so active producer/consumer generations do not alias. The slot from iteration `k - (MAX_LAG + 1)` is the one being overwritten at `k`; reuse is safe only after every consumer of that older generation has released it.\n\n**Safety condition**: This scheme is collision-free only when `MAX_LAG` is a structural upper bound on actual consumer retention and each slot region has exactly one producer before reuse. A producer at iteration `k + MAX_LAG + 1` must be gated by the last downstream consumer's release/consumed event for iteration `k`, not merely by producer-complete flags. If multiple downstream consumers read the same generation, reuse must wait for the aggregate/all-consumers release. If the schedule can let any consumer retain a slot beyond `MAX_LAG`, modulo rotation is unsafe.\n\n**Per-owner GM offset**: Use the producer/slot-owner core index for the workspace slice, not necessarily the current consumer core index.\n\n```cpp\nslotOffset = uint64_t(ownerCoreIdx) * SLOT_BYTES * (MAX_LAG + 1)\n           + uint64_t(slotIdx) * SLOT_BYTES;\n```\n\nFor same-core private staging, `ownerCoreIdx == coreIdx`. For peer handoff, consumers must address the producer/owner core's slot region.\n\n**Concrete anchor**:\n\n```cpp\nconstexpr uint32_t MAX_LAG = 2;\nconstexpr uint32_t SLOT_COUNT = MAX_LAG + 1;\n\nuint32_t writeSlot = iter % SLOT_COUNT;\nuint64_t writeOffset = uint64_t(ownerCoreIdx) * SLOT_BYTES * SLOT_COUNT\n                     + uint64_t(writeSlot) * SLOT_BYTES;\n\n// Consumer reads data produced `lag` iterations earlier.\n// Requires: 0 <= lag && lag <= MAX_LAG.\nuint32_t readSlot = (iter + SLOT_COUNT - lag) % SLOT_COUNT;\nuint64_t readOffset = uint64_t(ownerCoreIdx) * SLOT_BYTES * SLOT_COUNT\n                    + uint64_t(readSlot) * SLOT_BYTES;\n```\n\nTypical `MAX_LAG = 2` gives three slots per owner core and supports a three-generation overlapped window. `MAX_LAG = 1` reduces to classic double-buffer ping-pong. `MAX_LAG >= 3` is rarely useful because the cross-core flag chain and